ng_orbit) to the Moon between those dates, then the 600 km lunar orbit.",[462],[88,107,107],"SLIM","PRECISION LANDER · FLOWN","First precision landing within 100 m of target on another world","Until SLIM, lunar landings had a kilometre-scale targeting accuracy. JAXA's Smart Lander demonstrated 100 m accuracy — the precision required for crewed missions to dock with pre-positioned cargo, for sample-return ascent vehicles to find their orbital partners, and for any future architecture that depends on landing at specific scientifically valuable sites. SLIM also used a low-energy ballistic transfer to the Moon (Hiten pioneered it in 1991), taking 135 days to arrive but using less propellant than direct trajectories. A demonstration of the patience interplanetary missions can afford when timeline isn't the binding constraint.",[468,472,476,479,482,487],{"met":62,"label":469,"note":470,"type":471},"LAUNCH","H-IIA 202 from Tanegashima 2023-09-06.","nominal",{"met":51,"label":473,"note":474,"type":475},"BALLISTIC TRANSFER","Low-energy ballistic capture trajectory in progress.","info",{"met":88,"label":477,"note":478,"type":471},"LOI","Lunar orbit insertion.",{"met":12,"label":480,"note":481,"type":471},"TOUCHDOWN","Soft-landing at Shioli Crater 2024-01-19 15:20 UTC. Within target accuracy: about 10 m from the target.",{"met":483,"label":484,"note":485,"type":486},135.01,"TILTED LANDING","Lander resting nose-down due to engine anomaly during final descent. Solar panels initially in shadow.","warning",{"met":488,"label":489,"note":490,"type":475},144,"POWER RESUMED","Sun angle changes; solar panels generate power; the lander survived three lunar nights, with contact until April 2024.","/orrery/images/missions/slim/01.webp"],"uses":{"params":["id"]}}]}
